{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And he said: Adonai, if now I have found favor in Your sight.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): he called all of them 'lords' and said to the greatest among them, 'if now I have found favor in your sight,' and so the entire passage. And this interpretation follows the view of the one who says that it is secular (חול). But because we find it pointed with a qamatz in all the books, we must say that it is sacred (קדש), and he called them by a plural name." "<b>And it is fitting</b> to say that to Michael alone he addressed himself by the name of his Master, because he is the angel of lovingkindness (חסד) according to his attribute; therefore he began to speak with him and mentioned water at the beginning of his words and butter and milk at the end of his words, and no wine was mentioned in the meal, and this is clear. And the reason why this name is pointed with a qamatz when it is sacred (קדש) is well known." "<b>And according to</b> the plain sense of the wisdom of the vowel-points, the qamatz indicates a high rank and is first among the seven vowels, which are: qamatz, patach, tzere, segol, cholam, shureq, chiriq — for through the seven of them the Torah is built and established, and they are called the seven breaths, or seven voices, concerning which David said in the psalm of the giving of the Torah 'seven voices,' because all the Torah is founded upon them; and this is the plain sense of the saying 'in seven voices was it given,' and concerning them Solomon said (Proverbs 9:1): ״חצבה עמודיה שבעה״ — 'she has hewn out her seven pillars' — for they are the pillars upon which the entire house stands. And the difference between qamatz and patach is only in the point, for the superiority of the qamatz is only in the shape of the point, and the point alludes to the point of existence, which is the Holy Palace situated in the center, likened to a point. And therefore the point serves in seven aspects: when you place it above the letter it becomes cholam; in the middle of the letter, shureq; and with patach it becomes qamatz; and with chiriq it becomes tzere; and with tzere, segol; and with sheva it becomes qubbutz of the lips — behold, seven. And furthermore in the letters themselves: if you place the point in the letter heh it becomes chet; and in vav it becomes zayin; in kaf it becomes bet; and in nun it becomes gimel; and in resh it becomes dalet. And therefore one who adds a point or subtracts one destroys the world and cuts down the plantings of the Torah and their roots. And even though it may appear that the qamatz and the patach are all one thing and one vowel, it is not so, but there is a difference between them in pronunciation, for the vowel-sound of the qamatz is high and supernal, and the vowel-sound of the patach is below it. For the qamatz in a word indicates a high rank and something that stands on its own, not dependent on what follows — as in the case of ארון, which is with a qamatz, whereas we find ארון הברית which is with a patach; and likewise (Exodus 23:20): ״הנה אנכי שולח מלאך״ — with a patach — and (Numbers 20:16): ״וישלח מלאך״ — which is with a qamatz; and מלאך הברית is with a patach, because the patach is always dependent on what follows it, but not so the qamatz, for it stands on its own. And for this reason every etnachta and end of a verse is with a qamatz, for there it stands and does not lean. And for this reason, in pointing this name you will not find a patach but only a qamatz, for the supreme and great rank indicating supremacy and that He is not dependent upon another as a created thing is; and because of this it is written with alef-dalet when it is sacred with a qamatz, and when it is secular with a patach. And what is written in Lot's case — ״אל נא אדני״ — with a qamatz, though it is secular, is because it is the end of a verse. And this is the great distinction that exists in our Torah between qamatz and patach, like the difference between light and darkness and between sacred and secular. And likewise we find that one who substitutes a patach for a qamatz will not escape one of two things: either he will destroy the meaning, or he will come to heresy. For the letter heh of (Genesis 18:23): ״האף תספה״ — had you pointed it with a qamatz, you would have reversed the intent of Abraham and destroyed his thought; and the letter heh of (Job 8:3): ״האל יעות משפט״ — had you pointed it with a qamatz, it would be heresy. Besides this, the masters of grammar further said that the qamatz is said of something true, but not so the patach. Hence the verse says (II Samuel 1:10): ״ואעמד עליו ואמתתהו״ — and the vav of ואעמד you will find with a qamatz, but the vav of ואמתתהו with a patach, for what he said, ״ואעמד עליו״ — 'I stood upon him' — was true, but not so ואמתתהו — 'and I finished him off' — for Saul killed himself. And behold, all this is from the wisdom of our divine Torah and our holy language, and for this reason the letters were likened to a body and the point to a soul, for the points move the letters just as the soul moves the body, as our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded in Sefer ha-Bahir: 'the point in the letters of the Torah of Moses is like the soul of life in the body of a man.' And likewise they said in tractate Megillah: 'and this — all of it — the point.'" "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: the reason it is pointed with a qamatz is that the name itself is qametz — compounded from two holy names, each of four letters: one indicating primacy and unity, and one indicating emanation (atzilut); therefore the alef at the beginning and the yod at the end, and the attribute of judgment (din) in the middle." "<b>Know that</b> there are divine names, each of four letters, and three of them are mentioned in the Torah: the supernal source, the middle line, and the appellation. And all three are alluded to in the verse (Exodus 35:17): ״את קלעי החצר את עמודיו ואת אדניה״ — in the portion of VaYakhel; and likewise in the verse (Job 38:6): ״על מה אדניה הטבעו או מי ירה אבן פנתה״ — for the Tabernacle is a likeness of the creation of the world; and the fourth is alluded to in the matter of Rebekah. And understand this."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:13:22.413082117Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}